{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion lag: The model consistently fails to anticipate sharp downward breaks (e.g., 79.80 to 70.56) and instead projects a slow, linear decline. Apply an immediate downward adjustment of 5-8 EUR in the first 3-5 days following any history peak above 78 EUR.",
    "Adjust for volatility overshoot: The model underestimates the magnitude of subsequent rebounds after crashes. When true prices bottom out (e.g., near 66-67 EUR), the model's recovery trajectory is too flat; increase the forecast slope by 20-30% for days 15-25 post-crash.",
    "Sentiment-weighted bias correction: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history often precede immediate price drops rather than sustained rallies. When sentiment history shows a spike to 1.00 followed by a price drop, reduce the 30-day forecast by 3-4 EUR to account for the 'sell the news' effect.",
    "Horizon-specific floor adjustment: The model assumes a stable floor around 70-71 EUR, but true outcomes frequently breach this to 66-67 EUR. Lower the model's long-term mean reversion target by 3-4 EUR to reflect the actual lower bound observed during volatility.",
    "Trend reversal detection: The model treats short-term dips as temporary corrections within a stable range. If the 18-day history shows a drop of >4 EUR from the peak, override the model's linear projection with a steeper initial decline followed by a V-shaped recovery."
  ]
}
